WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  three years ago i was sitting at what i  thought to be the perfect job  i was making six figures working for a  tech company that provided free meals  unlimited vacation time off and yes it  did have a nap room  i thought i had reached the apex of the  american dream the american dream that  my parents immigrated from china and  vietnam to this country for  in hopes that their child would have a  chance at a better life than they did  but  it didn't feel that way  i read and watched the news every day  over 38 million americans are food  insecure  children are being forcibly separated  from their families at the border  texas school shooting florida school  shooting voting rights under attack  again  my friends and i would turn to each  other in frustration anger and sadness  did you see this  did you hear this  often  i cried  here i was living this picture-perfect  life but i knew back home in  philadelphia and across the country  people were struggling just to survive  in the neighborhood my parents first  immigrated to there are still people who  can't afford to keep a roof over their  heads  still people can't afford to put food on  the table  still children are getting asthma from  poor air quality just like i did  still in this country we call great  people are suffering and dying from  rooted injustices  the american dream rang hollow  so i quit my tech job  talking about these problems wasn't  going to make them go away  it wasn't enough to be frustrated angry  or disappointed everything i had that my  parents immigrated to this country for  meant little if there were people still  struggling like we once did  i decided to return home to philadelphia  to do the job i had done many years ago  as a student  community organizing  to me the most urgent problem our  country faced was that our democracy was  in crisis  so i joined a national organization  working to get out the vote  in that job i took a 60 pay cut and had  never been pushed to work harder in my  life  we were expected to work on holidays and  to quote sleep when we are dead  it was a far cry from the comfortable  silicon valley life i was living just a  few months ago  in that job i met and rediscovered  community organizers who have been doing  this work for decades in philadelphia  i first met tina marie two years ago  working to get out the vote in philly  the work we did was grueling but it was  made even more difficult by the pandemic  not everyone could vote in person  not everyone had internet access  and the people with power and resources  didn't seem to care  many times i wanted to bang my head  against a wall in frustration  but tina marie never gave up  she showed up to meetings she even used  her personal savings to help pay for her  neighbor's internet costs  tina marie is over 60 now  but she has the energy of a teenager for  over four decades she has been serving  her community  she spends her wednesdays attending  zoning board meetings which can go on  for six hours  she holds countless meetings for her  neighbors to hold developers accountable  demanding that they invest in home  ownership not just profit off of rent  tina marie does this work around the  clock  she works every day  into the evenings and on the weekends to  serve her community  and  she does this work for free  yes you heard that correctly  tina marie doesn't have a job that pays  her  serving her community is a full-time  role  why does she do it you may ask  because she says  if she doesn't do it  no one else will  her neighborhood  like many marginalized neighborhoods  across the country have faced the same  problems for decades lack of quality  housing education jobs  imagine  living paycheck to paycheck sending your  kids to schools with asbestos and being  surrounded by vacant lots overfilled  with trash  that's a reality that many americans in  this country still have to face today  and so you have people like tina marie  and tens of thousands of others fighting  for their communities around the clock  without pay and with little rest despite  all they have endured they are still  giving back  they won't leave  why  because they're fighting for something  deeper than money prestige or power  they're fighting for their home  when his neighbors didn't know how to  vote during the pandemic maurice  co-founded the philly voter engagement  network he built a team of hundreds of  people working to spread the vote about  mail-in ballot options and polling place  changes  when not enough was done to educate  students about voting tom co-founded pa  youth vote he built a coalition of  teachers in more than 40 schools  to become voter champions and empower  their students to take civic action when  nothing was done about gun violence in  her neighborhood kendra started stand up  nice town she brought a new resource to  her community every week to change the  conversation  like job readiness training and  counseling services  when an outside entity tried to take  over her neighborhood public school  kendra galvanized parents to vote for it  to be kept in the public's control  and it is through community organizing  that she won her seat in city council  still fighting for justice for her home  community organizing  is treated as volunteer work  but  it shouldn't be it's why we have a  five-day work week  it's why we have paid time off  it's why everyone in this room has the  right to vote  we stand here today because community  organizers fought for us  when this country was founded there was  no concept of overtime pay or minimum  wage child labor was not uncommon  but people unionized and fought  organizing major strikes to demand  better  their actions built the momentum for the  fair labor standards act banning  oppressive child labor and instituting  the work week we have today  it's time we stop taking community  organizing for granted and pay it  forward  i believe in people power  i believe that we need more grassroots  leaders who are from the communities  they serve people like tina marie tom  kendra and maurice people who won't give  up because this is their home  community organizing is often treated as  volunteer work like i said but it  shouldn't be  we should really focus on centering  these people in the movement for justice  it's easy to become complacent  to feel like everything is perfect  to do nothing  but i'm asking you for just a few  minutes  to think about where you sit in this  world  even right now here at stamford in this  very room today  take a look around  we are sitting in an institution with a  37 billion dollar endowment  stanford's highest paid employee makes  over 8 million dollars  hugging the western edge of campus is  sandhill road the epicenter of venture  capital and the most expensive street in  all of america  at the same time on el camino real which  hugs the northeastern edge of campus  dozens of rvs are parked  filled with people just trying to get by  abigail couldn't afford the 600 increase  from her landlord in rent so she and her  family of 10 moved into an rv  tanika and ivan moved into an rv when  the city started cracking down on people  living in cars despite both being  employed  they couldn't afford the rent  what may look perfect from your vantage  point  looks like hell to someone who doesn't  have that privilege  we were all raised to believe in the  american  dream but what does the american dream  mean  if we can't all have it  what does it mean when the barriers are  so high  only a few can access it  and what does it mean when we continue  to always leave the same people behind  community organizers are fighting for  affordable housing generational wealth  and voting rights because they know that  not everyone has access to it  living means being a part of the world  you live in  it means taking care of each other  it means standing up for each other  especially when no one else will  that is what the american dream should  look like  and so i'm asking you today  to care to make it better  to advocate for invest in and center the  voices of community organizers like tina  marie tom maurice and kendra  it's not about any one of us  it's about all of us  it's about what makes us human  caring for each other  so let's get to work together  [Applause]  [Music]  you

